WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.266
This video covers how to debug USB problems, how a Type C connection decides who is host, and how firmware gets updated over USB.

00:00:08.666 --> 00:00:20.311
USB Type-C: The reversible USB connector; it also negotiates power and roles through its extra pins. Like: A smart socket that works either way up and agrees on who powers whom before turning on.

00:00:20.611 --> 00:00:27.287
Firmware: Software that runs close to hardware, usually stored in flash memory on the device. Like: The instincts built into an animal: low-level behaviour that is there from birth.

00:00:28.337 --> 00:00:34.850
A customer escalation: the bar works on Windows laptops but drops off a particular Mac after ten minutes.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.978
Guessing won't fix it. Capturing the actual packets will.

00:00:39.228 --> 00:00:44.708
This video is your USB debugging toolbox, plus the Type C rules about who powers whom.

00:00:45.758 --> 00:00:49.020
Debugging USB is like investigating a phone call.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:52.206
The kernel log is one person's notes after the call.

00:00:52.606 --> 00:00:59.329
Kernel: The core of the operating system that controls memory, devices and which program runs. Like: The building manager who owns every key and decides who uses which room.

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:03.201
Usbmon with Wireshark lets you hear the host's side of every request.

00:01:03.601 --> 00:01:12.204
usbmon: A Linux feature that records every USB packet so you can read them in Wireshark. Like: A security camera pointed at the USB cable.

00:01:12.504 --> 00:01:17.055
A hardware analyzer is a wiretap on the cable itself, hearing both sides.

00:01:17.455 --> 00:01:22.552
And only the wiretap hears line noise: retries, chirps and power state changes.

00:01:23.752 --> 00:01:24.448
Words.

00:01:24.848 --> 00:01:28.564
X H C I is the standard USB three host controller.

00:01:28.964 --> 00:01:35.465
xHCI: Extensible Host Controller Interface: the standard USB host controller in laptops and in our boards when they act as host. Like: The hotel manager who calls guests, as opposed to the front desk that answers them.

00:01:35.765 --> 00:01:39.376
Usbmon captures USB traffic inside the host's kernel.

00:01:39.776 --> 00:01:47.903
Type C is the connector. Power delivery is a protocol on its C C wire that negotiates power, data roles and alternate modes.

00:01:48.303 --> 00:01:52.645
PD (Power Delivery): USB Power Delivery: the conversation over the Type-C cable that agrees on voltage, current and roles. Like: Two people agreeing how much to pay before handing over goods.

00:01:52.945 --> 00:02:03.719
CC pins: The configuration-channel wires in a Type-C cable used to detect orientation and talk Power Delivery. Like: The cable's private phone line for negotiating before the main road opens.

00:02:04.019 --> 00:02:15.617
Alternate mode: Using some Type-C wires to carry another signal, such as DisplayPort video, instead of USB data. Like: A multi-lane road where some lanes are temporarily given to buses.

00:02:15.917 --> 00:02:20.875
Dual role, or D R D, means one port can be either host or device.

00:02:21.275 --> 00:02:27.033
DRD / OTG: Dual-role device: one USB port that can switch between being host and being device. Like: A person who can be either the customer or the shopkeeper depending on who walks in.

00:02:27.333 --> 00:02:30.758
And D F U is the device firmware upgrade class.

00:02:31.158 --> 00:02:36.894
DFU: Device Firmware Upgrade: a standard way to send new firmware into a device over USB. Like: A car driving onto a service ramp so the mechanic can swap the engine software.

00:02:37.994 --> 00:02:39.666
Load usbmon on the host.

00:02:40.066 --> 00:02:42.086
Find which bus your device is on.

00:02:42.486 --> 00:02:45.620
Capture on that bus with tshark or Wireshark.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.075
Filter on your device's address.

00:02:48.475 --> 00:02:51.993
Filter on get descriptor requests to study enumeration.

00:02:52.393 --> 00:02:55.632
Descriptor: A small table of facts the device reports about itself: who made it, what it can do. Like: A business card plus resume the device hands over when it is first plugged in.

00:02:55.882 --> 00:03:03.127
Enumeration: The first conversation after plug-in where the laptop learns what the device is and gives it an address. Like: A new employee's first morning: show ID, get a badge number, hand in a resume, get assigned a desk.

00:03:03.377 --> 00:03:06.071
Or on isochronous transfers to study video.

00:03:06.471 --> 00:03:19.392
Isochronous transfer: A USB transfer type with guaranteed timing but no retries, used for live video and audio. Like: A live TV broadcast: always on time, and a dropped frame is simply skipped, never resent.

00:03:19.692 --> 00:03:23.826
On Windows use U S B pcap, and on a Mac use I O reg.

00:03:25.026 --> 00:03:26.825
A symptom table to memorize.

00:03:27.225 --> 00:03:34.342
Enumeration error minus seventy one: check PHY tuning, cable, V bus, and when D plus is pulled up.

00:03:34.742 --> 00:03:42.579
PHY: The physical-layer circuit that turns bits into electrical signals on the wire. Like: The voice box that turns your thoughts into sound waves.

00:03:42.879 --> 00:03:53.119
VBUS: The 5-volt power wire in a USB cable; seeing it tells the device a host is attached. Like: The doorbell wire: when it carries power, someone is at the door.

00:03:53.419 --> 00:04:02.080
Works on Linux but not Windows: check total length, string descriptors, Microsoft O S descriptors, and b c d Device caching.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:11.478
Video stutters only on some laptops: other devices sharing the host controller, an alternate setting that's too big, or link power management.

00:04:11.878 --> 00:04:16.440
Disconnects after suspend: remote wake up and runtime power management.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:22.750
Runtime PM / suspend: Turning parts of the device off when idle and back on when needed. Like: Motion-sensor lights for each room of the house.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.385
Charges but no data on Type C: the orientation mux, the dual role state, or the power contract.

00:04:30.585 --> 00:04:33.244
Now a Type C attach, step by step.

00:04:33.644 --> 00:04:39.867
The laptop presents a pull up resistor on the C C wire, which says: I'm a power source and a host.

00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:47.337
The PD controller sees which C C pin is connected, so it knows which way the plug is flipped, and sets the data mux.

00:04:47.737 --> 00:04:52.068
It negotiates a power contract, for example twenty volts at three amps.

00:04:52.468 --> 00:04:55.266
It tells the SoC: you are the device.

00:04:55.666 --> 00:05:01.471
SoC: System on a chip: one chip holding the processor cores, graphics, video, USB and more. Like: A whole city on one island: houses, factories and roads in one place.

00:05:01.771 --> 00:05:06.624
The dual role driver switches the controller into gadget mode, and starts the U D C.

00:05:07.024 --> 00:05:11.645
Driver: Kernel code that knows how to operate one kind of hardware. Like: A translator who speaks one specific machine's language.

00:05:11.895 --> 00:05:24.817
Gadget (Linux USB gadget): Linux's name for the device side of USB: the board pretends to be a webcam, mic or keyboard to a laptop. Like: An actor in costume: the same board can play "webcam" or "keyboard" depending on the costume it wears.

00:05:25.117 --> 00:05:36.610
UDC: USB Device Controller: the chip block that lets a gadget answer a laptop over USB. Like: The front desk of a hotel: every guest request (the laptop) arrives there first.

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:40.196
D plus is pulled up, and enumeration begins.

00:05:40.596 --> 00:05:46.006
Optionally, the two sides enter DisplayPort alternate mode to share a display on the same cable.

00:05:47.206 --> 00:05:49.842
Finally, a D F U firmware update.

00:05:50.242 --> 00:05:54.886
The host asks the device to switch into D F U mode, and it re enumerates.

00:05:55.286 --> 00:05:57.701
The host downloads the image in blocks.

00:05:58.101 --> 00:06:02.106
After each block, it polls status while the device writes flash.

00:06:02.506 --> 00:06:05.803
A zero length download means: that's everything.

00:06:06.203 --> 00:06:13.076
In the manifest phase, the device verifies the signature and version, and rejects bad or old images.

00:06:13.476 --> 00:06:19.235
Then it resets into the new image, or falls back to the old one. Lab nine simulates that.

00:06:20.435 --> 00:06:28.423
Question for you: The device disappears after exactly ten minutes on one laptop. Name one likely area and one tool to confirm it.

00:06:33.723 --> 00:06:43.208
Answer: Power management, such as the link going to sleep or the port suspending. Confirm with a USB packet capture and the kernel log at the moment it drops.

00:06:44.308 --> 00:06:45.005
Recap.

00:06:45.405 --> 00:06:49.224
Usbmon sees requests; an analyzer sees the wire.

00:06:49.624 --> 00:06:55.476
Use the symptom table: electrical, descriptors, bandwidth, power, roles.

00:06:55.876 --> 00:07:00.566
Type C goes: detect, negotiate, choose role, start the controller.

00:07:00.966 --> 00:07:04.879
And D F U is download, verify, reset, with a fallback.
